               "cons":  [
                            "The stock fell 15.3% in the week to Nov 20 and closed at $237.41, below its EMA 50, 100 and 200.",
                            "An AI-led correction (S\u0026P 500 −3.7% on Nov 20) hit \u0027AI power\u0027 names regardless of their results.",
                            "A 10-year Treasury yield near 5% and ~95% odds of a Fed hike on Dec 9 weigh on capital-heavy power stocks.",
                            "Policy risk is rising: PJM price caps and auction delays, and Texas data-center politics that touch Calpine\u0027s ERCOT fleet.",
                            "Q3 was partly held back by higher interest costs and a weaker nuclear production-tax-credit contribution."
                        ],
               "opportunityCheck":  "The scores look broadly reasonable. Position fits: Constellation is the largest US nuclear owner and keeps winning hyperscaler contracts. I would nudge AI revenue exposure up a little. Google, Amazon, Microsoft, Meta and the new Peak+ buyer now tie several gigawatts to data centers, though that is still a minority of a fleet that grew with Calpine. I would trim bottleneck \u0026 pricing power by a point or two. PJM price caps, FERC\u0027s procurement pause and Texas data-center politics show that regulators can limit what scarce clean power earns.",
               "technical":  "The trend is down. The price of $237.41 sits below the EMA 50 ($266.49), EMA 100 ($270.35) and EMA 200 ($278.57). EMAs are exponential moving averages that track the short-, medium- and long-term trend. They are stacked in downtrend order, with the 50 below the 100 and the 100 below the 200. On momentum, MACD (a trend-momentum gauge) is −2.68, below its signal line at −0.92, and the histogram is negative at −1.76. That means selling pressure is building again after the post-earnings bounce faded. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 37.46: weak, but not yet oversold. The price is sitting right on the lower Bollinger Band at $236.34. The bands sit about two standard deviations either side of the 20-day average of $261.59. Touching the lower band means selling is stretched, but in strong declines price can ride the band lower. The stock is 37.7% below its 52-week high of $380.78 and only 3.8% above its 52-week low of $228.63, which is the next support. On Nov 20 it fell 5.8% on 5.49M shares, about 1.4× the 20-day average, and closed near the day\u0027s low of $236.80. The heavy volume confirms the selling.",
               "fundamental":  "Constellation sells round-the-clock, carbon-free power, the scarce input hyperscalers need, and its contract book keeps growing. Q3, reported Nov 9, brought adjusted EPS of $5.06 versus $3.04 a year earlier and revenue of about $9.55B (+45%). The Calpine gas fleet, acquired in January 2026, helped. Guidance moved to $12.50–12.80 from $11.50–12.50. New contracts include Amazon (690 MW, 20 years, Sept 30) and Google (~890 MW of uprates plus 2,700 MW of load-following, Oct 6). A first \u0027Peak+\u0027 deal adds 270 MW from 2028. Constellation also agreed to buy the 609 MW Rhode Island State Energy Center for $715M. Uprates to existing reactors, not new builds, drive near-term growth, and the DOE loan lowers their cost. Nothing this period weakens the moat. The drop reflects valuation and macro conditions.",
               "news":  [
                            "Nov 9: Q3 adjusted EPS of $5.06 beat a consensus near $3.90, and FY2026 guidance was narrowed upward to $12.50–12.80. The stock rose 10.5% that day.",
                            "Nov 9: Constellation agreed to buy the 609 MW Rhode Island State Energy Center (gas, ISO New England) from Shell Energy for $715M, pending regulatory approval.",
                            "Oct 27: DOE gave a conditional commitment to guarantee up to $5.0B of loans for nuclear uprates, with a 30-year term at 0.375% above Treasuries.",
                            "Oct 6: Google signed a 20-year PPA supporting more than $4.3B of uprates across 11 units (~890 MW, first in 2028), plus a 15-year 2,700 MW load-following agreement in PJM.",
                            "Nov 16–20: The shares fell four straight sessions (−15.3% for the week) during an AI-led market correction. BMO raised its target to $335 (Outperform) that same week, and no company-specific filing explained the drop.",
                            "Nov 3–5: Texas elected a governor who campaigned on halting new data centers, and power producers sold off. This matters to Constellation because Calpine brought a large Texas (ERCOT) fleet."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The stock is trading on AI market mood, not on its own results. It gave back the whole Nov 9 earnings jump within about eight sessions. With the 10-year yield near 5% and futures pricing a Fed hike on Dec 9, capital-heavy power stocks with high valuations can keep de-rating. The 52-week low of $228.63 is the nearest support, and a close below it would mean fresh one-year lows. Policy risk is also building, from PJM price caps and procurement delays to Texas data-center politics. These could cap the pricing power the thesis relies on.",
               "opportunity":  "For a long-term holder, the gap between business news and the share price is unusually wide. Guidance rose, three hyperscaler-linked deals were signed in about six weeks, and a low-cost DOE loan backs uprate growth. Yet the stock is about 38% below its 52-week high. Contracts of 15–20 years lock in revenue that does not depend on AI stock sentiment. The lower Bollinger Band and an RSI approaching 30 are zones where selling often runs out. However, the MACD has not turned up yet, so the chart has not confirmed a bottom.",
               "risks":  [
                             "The AI-led market correction deepens and drags AI power names further regardless of results.",
